#### 6. Magic Number Test — `CalculateTotal_ReturnsCorrectAmount` (line 70)

Three unexplained literals (`247.50`, `22.28`, `269.78`) appear in assertions. A reader cannot tell what prices or tax rate produce these values.

```csharp
// ❌ Smell
Assert.AreEqual(247.50m, order.TotalAmount);
Assert.AreEqual(22.28m,  order.TaxAmount);
Assert.AreEqual(269.78m, order.GrandTotal);
```

**Fix** — use named constants or compute expected values transparently:

```csharp
const decimal sku1UnitPrice = 59.99m;
const int sku1Qty = 3;
const decimal sku2UnitPrice = 67.53m;
const int sku2Qty = 1;
const decimal taxRate = 0.09m;

var expectedSubtotal = (sku1UnitPrice * sku1Qty) + (sku2UnitPrice * sku2Qty);
var expectedTax = Math.Round(expectedSubtotal * taxRate, 2);

Assert.AreEqual(expectedSubtotal, order.TotalAmount);
Assert.AreEqual(expectedTax, order.TaxAmount);
Assert.AreEqual(expectedSubtotal + expectedTax, order.GrandTotal);
```

**Risk if unfixed:** When prices or tax rules change, you can't tell whether the test or the production code is wrong.

